# config/Kconfig.network — Ethernet, TCP/IP stack parameters, ARP, brain link
#
# All network constants with defaults matching the
# current codebase values from crates/net/net/src/*.

menu "Network"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ethernet / IP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config TCP_BUF_SIZE
    int "TCP per-connection receive and send ring (bytes)"
    range 8192 1048576
    default 16384  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16384  if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 131072
    help
      Size of each of the two static rings of one TCP connection, the
      receive ring and the send ring (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs rx_buf and
      tx_buf).  Must be a power of two, and at least 8192 so that the
      send ring holds a few full-size segments of headroom for loss
      recovery; crates/core/limits/build.rs rejects anything else.

      Read from .config: tcp.rs TCP_BUF_SIZE = azos_limits::TCP_BUF_SIZE.
      Derived from it in tcp.rs: the send ring (TCP_SND_BUF_SIZE), the
      window of a SYN and of an unscaled connection
      (min(TCP_BUF_SIZE - 1, 65535)), the window-scale shift a SYN offers
      (the smallest that covers TCP_BUF_SIZE - 1: 1 at 131072, 0 at 65536
      or less), the initial ssthresh and the cwnd cap.

      Per profile: 131072 (128 KiB) for edge and embedded, 16384 (16 KiB)
      for fleet.  One connection is size_of::<TcpConn>() =
      2 × TCP_BUF_SIZE + 6,136 B, and the table of TCP_MAX_CONNS of them
      is static (.bss): 8 × 268,280 B for edge, 4 × 268,280 B for
      embedded, 1024 × 38,904 B = 38.0 MiB for fleet (262 MiB at 131072).
      Larger rings improve throughput
      on high-latency links (BDP = RTT × bandwidth).

      Embedded's memory is not budgeted: there is no embedded linker
      script and no gate build row, and the measured image is 5.6 MiB
      against the < 1 MiB SRAM PROFILE_EMBEDDED states.  131072 stays on
      embedded until that budget (target board and RAM, linker script,
      gate build row), which is queued, exists.

      At fleet's 16384 the shift this end offers is 0, so a two-node run
      of a fleet build prints wscale=<peer>/0.

      Cost: 2 x TCP_MAX_CONNS B of RAM per byte of ring (16 B per byte at
      edge's 8 connections: 2 MiB of the 2.05 MiB TCP table is rings), the
      same on riscv64 and aarch64 (measured: +2,097,152 B for 131072 -> 262144
      at 8 connections).  See TCP_MAX_CONNS for the per-connection and total
      figures.  Ceiling: a power of two in 8192..1,048,576 (build.rs refuses
      anything else).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TCP acknowledgements (RFC 1122 4.2.3.2, RFC 5681 4.2) and receive-path waits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config TCP_DELACK_MS
    int "TCP delayed-ACK bound (ms); 0 = acknowledge every segment at once"
    range 0 500
    default 40
    help
      Longest an in-order data segment's acknowledgement may be held
      (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs, TCP_DELACK_TICKS). An ACK held this
      long is sent from tcp_tick; one the receive path sends earlier
      (TCP_DELACK_SEGS, TCP_DELACK_PASS_FLUSH, out-of-order data, a
      closing window) or one that rides on our own data leaves sooner.
      40 ms is Linux's minimum delayed-ACK timeout (TCP_DELACK_MIN);
      RFC 1122 caps it at 500 ms and RFC 5681 at "less than 500 ms".

      0 restores the pre-wave-15 behaviour, one pure ACK per in-order
      segment, and is the canary for the coalescing.

      Cost: 16 B per connection (deadline + counters, .bss); a pure ACK
      saved is a frame build, a TX lock and a doorbell (~1,000-2,000
      instructions on both ISAs). The same on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64:
      the bound is a protocol figure, not an ISA one. Lower it when a peer
      runs Nagle with small writes and no TCP_NODELAY and
      TCP_DELACK_PASS_FLUSH is n (each small write then waits this long
      for its ACK).

config TCP_DELACK_SEGS
    int "Full-sized segments received before an immediate ACK"
    range 1 16
    default 2
    help
      RFC 1122 4.2.3.2 / RFC 5681 4.2: an ACK SHOULD be generated for at
      least every second full-sized segment. Once this many SMSS of
      in-order data are unacknowledged the receive path sends the ACK
      at once (tcp.rs, process_inbound_payload). 2 is the RFC and Linux
      figure; 1 acknowledges every full-sized segment (the sender's
      slow start grows fastest, twice the ACK frames). Higher values
      cut ACK frames further but slow a sender's congestion window
      growth (RFC 3465 byte counting is capped at 2 SMSS per ACK).

      Cost: none in RAM; one compare per in-order segment.

config TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MIN
    int "Lowest local port a TCP connect may pick"
    range 1024 65535
    default 49152
    help
      A connect that names no local port (NODE_CONNECT, the brain link,
      the camera link) takes a free one from
      TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MIN..TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MAX (tcp.rs,
      TcpLayer::ephemeral_port). A port is free while no connection
      that is not Closed holds it, TIME-WAIT included, and a cursor
      seeded from boot entropy walks the range so two consecutive
      connects never reuse a 4-tuple. 49152..65535 is the IANA dynamic
      range (RFC 6335); Linux defaults to 32768..60999.

      Cost: no RAM; one connection-table scan per port tried, at
      connect only. Lower it to widen the range for a fleet node that
      dials many peers; keep it above every port a service listens on.
      Same default on every ISA and profile.

config TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MAX
    int "Highest local port a TCP connect may pick"
    range 1024 65535
    default 65535
    help
      Upper end of the ephemeral range (see TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MIN);
      must not be below it (crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs asserts this at
      compile time). Cost: none.

config TCP_DELACK_PASS_FLUSH
    bool "Flush held ACKs at the end of each network poll pass"
    default y
    help
      y: every ACK held during one net_poll pass (one RX drain) leaves
      when the pass ends, one per connection, so ACK latency is at most
      one pass and a burst of N segments costs about N / 2 pure ACKs.
      n: held ACKs wait for our own data to carry them, for
      TCP_DELACK_SEGS, or for TCP_DELACK_MS (the Linux behaviour; a
      request/response peer then gets the ACK on our reply).

      Cost: one connection-table scan per pass while any ACK is held
      (skipped by a flag otherwise). Same default on every ISA and
      profile until a board measurement says otherwise.

config TCP_WINDOW_UPDATE_SHIFT
    int "Window update sent at once when the edge moves by ring >> N"
    range 0 8
    default 3 if TCP_BUF_SIZE < 32768
    default 2
    help
      crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs, TCP_WINDOW_UPDATE_BYTES = TCP_BUF_SIZE >> N.
      A recv() that opens the receive window by at least this much sends
      the window update as a pure ACK at once; a smaller opening is held
      like a delayed ACK (TCP_DELACK_MS) and rides on the next data ACK
      or the end of the next poll pass. Linux's half-window rule
      (tcp_cleanup_rbuf) also applies.

      Larger N: more, earlier updates; a sender limited by our ring
      (BDP > TCP_BUF_SIZE) keeps more of it in flight. Smaller N: fewer
      pure ACKs. Measured on the host stack-to-stack harness (20 ms RTT,
      ring-limited, tests/host/net-tests tcp_throughput), B/RTT against
      acknowledging every segment (TCP_DELACK_MS=0): 128 KiB ring, N=2:
      98.8%; 64 KiB: 97.7%; 32 KiB: 95.4% (N=3 the same); 16 KiB (fleet):
      N=2 72.9% (a 4 KiB step is under three segments of an eleven-segment
      window, and the held update stalls the sender), N=3 100% for 0.90
      pure ACKs per data segment against 1.36. Hence 3 below a 32 KiB ring.

      Cost: none in RAM; one compare per recv() that returns data. The
      same on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64.

config NET_WAIT_SLOTS
    int "Tasks that can wait on one kind of network event at once"
    range 1 64
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 8
    help
      crates/net/net/src/wait.rs, NET_WAIT_SLOTS: a task waiting for a TCP
      handshake, an incoming connection, the send window (one queue) or an
      ARP reply (another) registers its TID here and is woken by the
      receive path when the event lands, instead of sleeping 1 ms and
      looking again. A waiter beyond this many falls back to that poll;
      nothing is lost, only latency and wakes.

      Cost: 4 B per slot per queue (.bss, 2 queues + 1 DNS slot: 68 B at
      8), the same on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64. A notify with no waiter
      is one atomic load; with waiters, one scan of the slots and a
      scheduler wake per waiter (~300-500 instructions each).

config NET_DNS_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long one DNS query waits for its answer (ms)"
    range 100 30000
    default 2000
    help
      crates/net/net/src/dns.rs, resolve_with_yield: the bound on one
      query (was a literal 2 s). The resolver is woken by the answer
      (NET_WAIT_SLOTS), so this is only what an unanswered query costs
      its caller.

      Cost: none in RAM. The same on every ISA; raise it on slow uplinks.

config NTP_CLIENT
    bool "SNTP client task: keep the wall clock steered from a time server"
    default y
    help
      Starts the kernel's `net-clients` task (kernel/src/tasks/net_clients.rs),
      which asks a time server for the time and hands each answer to the clock
      discipline (azos_syscall::clock, source NTP): the first one sets a clock
      nobody set, later ones are slewed in or, past the discipline's
      thresholds, stepped or refused. The server is the address a DHCP lease
      names in option 42, or else the one NTP_SERVER names. Off: nothing asks
      for the time and the wall clock is the RTC's, or a PTP daemon's.

      Cost when set: one task (a 16 KiB stack) that sleeps between exchanges,
      and one UDP request of 48 bytes every NTP_INTERVAL_S. The same on
      riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64.

config NTP_SERVER
    string "Time server: a name or a dotted-quad IPv4 address (empty: only a lease names one)"
    default "time.cloudflare.com"
    help
      A name is resolved through the stack's resolver (the DHCP-given DNS
      server, else 8.8.8.8) before each exchange (the resolver keeps an answer
      five minutes), so a pool or an anycast name that moves is followed. A
      dotted quad is used as it is. A server named by a DHCP lease (option 42)
      replaces this one for as long as the lease says. Empty: no request is
      sent until a lease names a server. At most 48 characters (the Net
      interface's name limit).

      The default is a public anycast service, so a build that boots on a
      network with a route out starts talking to it; set the empty string for
      an isolated deployment, or the address of your own server.

config NTP_INTERVAL_S
    int "Seconds between time exchanges once synchronised"
    range 64 86400
    default 1024
    help
      The poll period of the NTP client task. RFC 4330 §10 asks a client not
      to poll faster than every 15 s, and a public server not faster than 64;
      the clock discipline estimates the counter's frequency error from
      samples this far apart, so a longer period gives it a longer baseline and
      a slower correction of a drifting oscillator. A failed exchange is retried
      after 16 s, doubling up to this period.

      Cost: none in RAM; one 48-byte request per period.

config TCP_RECV_MAX_PER_CALL
    int "Most bytes one TCP recv() copies out"
    range 512 65536
    default 1024 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4096
    help
      crates/net/net/src/tcp.rs, TCP_RECV_MAX_PER_CALL: the copy out of
      the receive ring runs with the TCP lock held, so this bounds that
      hold (a short read is ordinary TCP; callers loop).

      Cost: none in RAM; up to this many byte copies under the TCP lock
      per call (~1 instruction per byte on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64).
      Raise it for bulk receivers on a core with no other network user.

config NET_RX_DRAIN_PER_POLL
    int "Frames drained from the NIC per net_poll pass"
    range 1 1024
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64
    help
      crates/net/net/src/lib.rs, net_poll: the most frames one pass takes
      off the receive ring before it ends (and flushes held ACKs, see
      TCP_DELACK_PASS_FLUSH). 64 is one line-rate burst; a smaller bound
      shortens the pass on a small core at the price of more passes.

      Cost: none in RAM; bounds the pass at about this many frames' worth
      of protocol work (~2,000-5,000 instructions each on riscv64,
      aarch64 and x86_64).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Interfaces, routes and packet sockets (NET-IFS)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config NET_MAX_INTERFACES
    int "Network interfaces the stack can hold"
    range 1 8
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/ip.rs, MAX_IFS: the interface registry (ifindex 1
      is the primary one; named eth0, eth1, ... in registration order), and
      with it the number of ARP caches and outstanding-request tables
      (crates/net/net/src/arp.rs). An interface is one NIC the driver
      layer reports ready; NICs beyond this are left unused.

      Cost: 40 B per interface (the registry, .bss) plus one ARP cache and
      one outstanding-request table each (ARP_CACHE_SIZE, ARP_PENDING_SIZE):
      576 B per interface at their defaults, the same on riscv64, aarch64
      and x86_64. The static routes (TOPOLOGY_MAX_ROUTES) take 20 B each in
      the routing table. A route lookup scans them all.

config NET_PACKET_SOCKETS
    int "Raw layer-2 (AF_PACKET) sockets open at once"
    range 0 8
    default 1 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 2
    help
      crates/net/net/src/socket.rs, PACKET_SOCKETS: how many AF_PACKET
      sockets can exist at once, kernel-owned only for now (a gPTP or LLDP
      service). Each owns a receive ring of NET_PACKET_RX_SLOTS frames;
      0 refuses the socket type (the receive pass then costs one relaxed
      load per frame for it).

      Cost: 6 B + NET_PACKET_RX_SLOTS x 1,518 B of RAM per socket (.bss),
      the same on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64: 12,156 B at the defaults.

config NET_PACKET_RX_SLOTS
    int "Frames queued per AF_PACKET socket"
    range 1 64
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/socket.rs, PACKET_RX_SLOTS: the receive ring of each
      packet socket. A frame that arrives with the ring full is counted and
      dropped (the oldest stays): a reader that falls behind loses the
      newest traffic, as a UDP socket does.

      Cost: 1,518 B of RAM per slot per socket (.bss), see
      NET_PACKET_SOCKETS.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARP, DNS and UDP tables (wave 11: were literals in crates/net/net)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config ARP_CACHE_SIZE
    int "ARP cache entries"
    range 4 256
    default 64 if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16
    help
      crates/net/net/src/arp.rs, ARP_CACHE_SIZE. USE_GATEWAY on the
      Edge profile starts it at 64.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry per interface (ARP_TABLES, .bss: one cache
      for each of NET_MAX_INTERFACES), the same on riscv64 and aarch64: 400 B
      per interface at the default 16.

config ARP_PENDING_SIZE
    int "Outstanding ARP requests remembered"
    range 2 64
    default 16 if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 8
    help
      crates/net/net/src/arp.rs, ARP_PENDING_SIZE: requests whose reply
      is still eligible to be accepted. USE_GATEWAY on the Edge
      profile starts it at 16.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry per interface (ARP_PENDING, .bss: one table
      for each of NET_MAX_INTERFACES), the same on riscv64 and aarch64: 136 B
      per interface at the default 8.

config DNS_CACHE_SIZE
    int "DNS cache entries"
    range 1 64
    default 16 if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/dns.rs, DNS_CACHE_SIZE. USE_GATEWAY on the
      Edge profile starts it at 16.

      Cost: 80 B of RAM per entry (DNS_CACHE, .bss), the same on riscv64 and
      aarch64: 328 B at the default 4.

config UDP_RX_SLOTS
    int "Datagrams queued per UDP socket"
    range 2 64
    default 16 if USE_GATEWAY && PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4
    help
      crates/net/net/src/udp.rs, UDP_RX_SLOTS: the receive ring of each
      UDP socket. USE_GATEWAY on the Edge profile starts it at 16.
      Must be a power of two (the ring index is a mask);
      crates/core/limits/build.rs refuses any other value.

      Cost: 11,904 B of RAM per slot (the UDP table, .bss), the same on riscv64
      and aarch64: 8 UDP sockets (UDP_MAX_SOCKETS, a literal) x one 1,488-byte
      datagram slot each.  47,912 B for the whole UDP table at the default 4
      slots; 64 slots (the range limit) would be 761,856 B of slots.  It does not
      depend on MAX_SOCKETS.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firewall (crates/net/net/src/filter.rs, installed by kernel/src/boot/net.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config FIREWALL_INBOUND_DROP
    bool "Firewall: drop inbound packets no rule accepts"
    default y if USE_GATEWAY
    default n
    help
      The default policy of the packet filter for packets addressed to this
      node (and for packets it would route, the forward hook): what happens
      to a packet no `[firewall.NAME]` row of CAPS.TOML matches. Off: it is
      accepted. On: it is dropped unless it belongs to a TCP or UDP flow this
      node opened, or one an accepted rule opened, and the peer has answered.
      Outbound packets are accepted by default either way; a row can still
      refuse them. ICMP is stateless: a gateway that wants echo replies or
      "unreachable" errors back declares a row for them, and so for the DHCP
      reply (udp port 68) and the IGMP queries (proto any, the router).

      On by default under USE_GATEWAY. With this off and no row that refuses
      anything the filter is not installed at all and the stack pays one
      branch per packet.

      Cost: none in RAM or code size of its own; the tables below.

config FIREWALL_CONNTRACK_MAX
    int "Firewall: tracked TCP/UDP flows"
    range 16 8192
    default 64 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024 if USE_GATEWAY
    default 256
    help
      Size of the flow table the firewall keeps so that the answer to a
      packet this node sent (or to one a rule let in) passes whatever the
      rules say (crates/net/net/src/filter.rs, CT_SLOTS). The table is 4-way
      set-associative, keyed by the 5-tuple with a boot-random seed: the
      value is rounded down to a multiple of 4, and a flow whose set is full
      of live entries is refused, never the table grown. Within a set a
      new flow evicts, in this order, an expired entry, then the oldest one
      that has not been answered (or is closing), so a flood of new flows
      cannot push out an established one. The table is kept only when a rule or
      the inbound policy can refuse something a reply must still pass.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry (the filter's table, .bss), the same on
      riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64: 6,144 B at the default 256.

config FIREWALL_UDP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Firewall: idle timeout of a tracked UDP flow (s)"
    range 1 3600
    default 60
    help
      A UDP flow with no packet in either direction for this long is
      forgotten: its answers are judged by the rules again. Every packet in
      either direction renews it.

      Cost: none.

config FIREWALL_TCP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Firewall: idle timeout of an established TCP flow (s)"
    range 30 86400
    default 600 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 3600
    help
      An established TCP flow with no packet in either direction for this
      long is forgotten. Set it above the keepalive interval of the
      applications on the node, or a quiet connection stops passing inbound
      once its entry is gone. A flow that was never answered, or that has
      carried a FIN or an RST, is held for 20 s regardless (filter.rs,
      HALF_OPEN_S).

      Cost: none.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Forwarding and NAT (crates/net/net/src/ip.rs `forward`, nat.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config NET_IP_FORWARD
    bool "Forward IPv4 packets between interfaces"
    default y if USE_GATEWAY
    default n
    help
      On: a unicast packet that arrives on one interface for an address no
      interface of this node holds is routed (the route table of ip.rs) and
      sent out of the interface the route names: TTL decremented, header
      checksum rewritten, the packet filter asked at its forward hook. A
      packet with TTL 1 draws ICMP "time exceeded", one the egress link
      cannot carry ICMP "fragmentation needed" (the stack does not fragment),
      one nothing routes ICMP "network unreachable". A packet that would
      leave by the interface it arrived on is refused (no hairpin, no
      redirect). Broadcasts and multicast are never forwarded. Off: such a
      packet is dropped, as on a host.

      On by default under USE_GATEWAY, off elsewhere. Needs at least two
      interfaces (NET_MAX_INTERFACES) to do anything.

      Cost: none in RAM; about 1.5 KB of stack on the receive path, only
      while a packet is being forwarded.

config NET_NAT
    bool "NAPT: masquerade forwarded traffic behind the uplink"
    depends on NET_IP_FORWARD
    default y if USE_GATEWAY
    default n
    help
      The uplink is the interface of the best default route (a `[route.NAME]`
      row for 0.0.0.0/0 or a lease gateway). A packet forwarded from another
      interface out of the uplink leaves with the uplink's address as source
      and a port from NAT_PORT_MIN..NAT_PORT_MAX in place of the sender's;
      the answer that arrives on the uplink for that port from the same
      remote is translated back and forwarded to the sender. ICMP errors
      about a translated flow are translated back too. Only a TCP SYN, a UDP
      datagram or an ICMP echo request opens a mapping; nothing arriving from
      outside opens one (there is no port forwarding), and a LAN host cannot
      reach the uplink address through the gateway (hairpin refused).
      Protocols other than TCP, UDP and ICMP echo are refused.

      A mapping is per 5-tuple, so two flows of one LAN host never share a
      translated port; one more than the table or its set can hold is refused,
      never the table grown. Every refusal reaches the packet filter's
      record (Rule::PacketRefused).

      On by default under USE_GATEWAY. Packets are not fragmented or
      reassembled, so a fragment is dropped before it gets here.

      Cost: the table below, plus about 1.5 KB of stack while a packet is
      translated.

config NAT_TABLE_MAX
    int "NAT: tracked mappings"
    range 16 8192
    default 1024 if NET_NAT
    default 16
    help
      Size of the mapping table (crates/net/net/src/nat.rs, NAT_SLOTS). 4-way
      set-associative, keyed by the 5-tuple with a boot-random seed: the value
      is rounded down to a multiple of 4. The translated port of a mapping is
      chosen so that both directions of it live in the same set, which makes
      each lookup one set (4 entries) in either direction. A flow whose set
      is full of live answered mappings is refused. Within a set a new flow
      evicts, in this order, an expired mapping, then the oldest one nobody
      has answered (or that is closing). The table is static whatever
      NET_NAT is; with NAT off it stays at the minimum.

      Needs NAT_PORT_MIN..NAT_PORT_MAX to hold at least 4 ports per set
      (nat.rs asserts it at compile time).

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry (.bss), the same on riscv64, aarch64 and
      x86_64: 384 B at the minimum (NAT off), 24,576 B at 1024 (NAT on).

config NAT_PORT_MIN
    int "NAT: lowest translated port"
    range 1024 65535
    default 32768
    help
      Translated ports (and ICMP echo identifiers) come from
      NAT_PORT_MIN..NAT_PORT_MAX. The range must not overlap the TCP
      ephemeral range (TCP_EPHEMERAL_PORT_MIN..MAX): a reply to a connection
      of this node must never be taken for a translated one (nat.rs asserts
      it at compile time). 32768..49151 sits just below the default TCP range.

      Cost: none.

config NAT_PORT_MAX
    int "NAT: highest translated port"
    range 1024 65535
    default 49151
    help
      Upper end of the translated range (see NAT_PORT_MIN). Cost: none.

config NAT_UDP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "NAT: idle timeout of a UDP mapping (s)"
    range 1 3600
    default 30
    help
      A UDP mapping with no packet in either direction for this long is
      forgotten. Every packet in either direction renews it. DNS and NTP
      finish in a second; raise it for long-lived UDP such as a VPN keepalive
      (RFC 4787 asks for at least 2 minutes there).

      Cost: none.

config NAT_TCP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "NAT: idle timeout of an established TCP mapping (s)"
    range 60 86400
    default 600 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 7440
    help
      An answered TCP mapping with no packet in either direction for this
      long is forgotten (RFC 5382 REQ-5: no less than 2 h 4 min by default).
      Set it above the keepalive interval of the applications behind the
      gateway, or a quiet connection loses its mapping.

      Cost: none.

config NAT_TCP_TRANSIENT_S
    int "NAT: timeout of an unanswered or closing TCP mapping (s)"
    range 5 300
    default 30
    help
      A TCP mapping nobody has answered, or that has carried a FIN or an RST,
      is held this long without a packet, so the closing exchange still
      passes and a half-open flood frees its entries quickly.

      Cost: none.

config NAT_ICMP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "NAT: idle timeout of an ICMP echo mapping (s)"
    range 1 3600
    default 30
    help
      An ICMP echo identifier stays mapped this long without a packet.

      Cost: none.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NIC queues (wave 15 IO-QUEUES N1/N2/N3: were VIRTIO_QUEUE_SIZE = 16 and
# literals in crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/net.rs, crates/net/net/src/lib.rs
# and kernel/src/tasks/net_poll.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config NET_VIRTIO_RXQ_SIZE
    int "virtio-net RX ring entries (= posted RX buffers)"
    range 2 256
    default 16  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/net.rs NUM_RX_BUFS: the RX virtqueue
      size requested from the device (it may grant fewer), and the number
      of receive buffers posted on it, one descriptor each.  A power of two
      (split-ring index arithmetic) and at most 256 (one 4 KiB page of
      descriptors); crates/core/limits/build.rs refuses anything else.

      What it buys: frames the NIC holds while nothing drains it.  In poll
      mode a 13 ms wake gap was measured (kernel/src/tasks/net_poll.rs):
      64 buffers carry ~4,900 frames/s across it, 16 carry ~1,200 (the old
      8 carried ~615).  Raise it for a receive-heavy node.

      Cost: 1,526 B of RAM per entry (.bss, the same on every ISA) plus the
      page-backed ring: 97,664 B of buffers at edge's 64, 24,416 B at
      embedded's 16, 390,656 B at fleet's 256 (was 12,208 B: 8 buffers).
      Instructions per frame do not depend on it.

config NET_VIRTIO_TXQ_SIZE
    int "virtio-net TX ring entries (2 per frame in flight)"
    range 4 256
    default 16  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 64
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/net.rs NUM_TX_BUFS: the TX virtqueue
      size requested from the device.  Each frame takes two descriptors
      (virtio-net header, then the frame), so half of it is frames in
      flight; a send that finds the ring full is refused (QueueFull) and
      counted.  A power of two, at most 256 (build.rs checks both).

      Cost: 1,514 B of RAM per entry (.bss: one staging buffer per
      descriptor index): 96,896 B at edge's 64, 24,224 B at embedded's 16
      (the old size), 387,584 B at fleet's 256.  Lower it on a node that
      only sends control traffic; raise it with NET_TX_BATCH_MAX for bulk
      TCP senders.

config NET_TX_BATCH_MAX
    int "virtio-net TX frames published per doorbell inside a batch"
    range 1 128
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16
    help
      Inside a TX batch (one net_poll pass, one TCP send_all_* burst
      between waits) frames are published on the TX ring without ringing
      the doorbell; it rings once when the batch ends, or after this many
      frames, whichever comes first.  A send outside any batch rings at
      once, as before, so no frame waits for a flush point that is not
      coming.  Either way the doorbell is skipped while the device reports
      it is already draining the ring (used.flags NO_NOTIFY, virtio 1.x
      2.7.10; the legacy transport defines it too).

      1 = one doorbell per frame (the behaviour before wave 15).  At most
      NET_VIRTIO_TXQ_SIZE / 2, the frames the ring holds (build.rs checks
      it), or a batch could fill the ring before announcing it.

      Cost: nothing in RAM.  A doorbell is an MMIO store that exits to the
      device model (QEMU) or crosses the bus (hardware); a larger batch
      trades that per-frame cost for up to this many frames of added
      latency inside one burst.

config NET_RX_BATCH_MAX
    int "virtio-net RX frames popped per lock and handed up by reference"
    range 1 128
    default 4  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/net.rs, recv_batch: one lock pops up
      to this many received frames, the lock is dropped while the stack
      handles them in place (no copy out of the RX buffer), and one more
      lock re-posts the buffers. 1 = a lock pair per frame.

      At most NET_VIRTIO_RXQ_SIZE / 2 (build.rs checks it): the popped
      buffers are off the ring until the batch has been handled, and the
      device needs the other half to keep receiving meanwhile.

      Cost: 4 B of stack per entry in recv_batch; buffers out of the ring
      for one batch's protocol work. Same on every ISA.

config NET_VIRTIO_EVENT_IDX
    bool "virtio-net: negotiate VIRTIO_F_EVENT_IDX on modern transports"
    default y
    help
      crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/net.rs: offer VIRTIO_F_EVENT_IDX
      (virtio 1.x 2.7.7) on virtio-mmio version 2 and virtio-pci. The
      driver then asks for its next interrupt by ring index (used_event)
      and rings the doorbell only when avail.idx passes the device's
      avail_event, instead of the NO_INTERRUPT / NO_NOTIFY flags. The
      legacy virtio-mmio transport keeps the flags either way.

      Cost: no RAM; two 16-bit reads/writes of the ring per doorbell or
      interrupt decision, where the flags took one. Same on every ISA and
      profile; turn it off to compare doorbell and interrupt counts
      (`ifconfig` queues line) or for a device whose EVENT_IDX is broken.

config NET_RX_IRQ
    bool "NIC RX interrupt instead of timer polling"
    default y
    help
      On the virtio-mmio NIC (QEMU virt on riscv64 and aarch64, microvm on
      x86_64) take the transport's interrupt line (PLIC/APLIC source, GIC
      SPI, IOAPIC GSI) and wake the net poll task only on traffic.  The
      VisionFive 2's Synopsys MAC (gmac0, PLIC source 7) does the same:
      its handler acknowledges the DMA channel and opens the RX gate.
      A link change is noticed by the next pass, so with this on and no
      traffic and no TCP deadline it can take up to NET_IRQ_IDLE_CEILING_MS.
      For the virtio NIC the handler acknowledges the device and opens the RX gate; the pass
      switches RX interrupts off while it drains (avail.flags
      NO_INTERRUPT) and back on once the ring is empty, then looks at the
      ring once more so a frame landing in between is not missed.  TX
      completions interrupt only while the TX ring is full.  The
      virtio-pci NIC with an MSI route was already interrupt-driven.

      n = the poll task wakes every NET_POLL_PERIOD_US whatever the
      traffic.  Either way TCP timers run at their own deadlines.  Cost of y: an interrupt per RX burst instead of a timer
      wake per period; a missed interrupt is bounded by
      NET_IRQ_IDLE_CEILING_MS.

config NET_TX_BATCH_SELFCHECK
    bool "Boot self-check of virtio-net TX doorbell batching"
    default y if CANARY_RUNTIME
    help
      kernel/src/boot/net.rs: right after the NIC comes up, open a TX batch,
      queue three broadcast frames of the local experimental EtherType
      0x88B5 (dropped by any receiver), and check that no doorbell decision
      was taken inside the batch and exactly one at its end.  Prints
      `[NET] TX batch self-check: ... PASS` (or FAIL), which the gate rows
      `network: TX doorbell batching (rv|arm)` read; the runtime canary
      `canary=net-kick-per-frame` turns it to FAIL.

      Cost: three 60-byte frames on the wire at boot and a few hundred
      instructions, once.  On by default where runtime canaries are
      (development images); n in product images.

config NET_POLL_PERIOD_US
    int "net poll task period while the NIC is polled (us)"
    range 100 1000000
    default 1000
    help
      kernel/src/tasks/net_poll.rs (was TIMER_FREQ / 1000): how often the
      net poll task drains the NIC and runs the TCP timers when there is no
      RX interrupt.  Cost: one task wake per period; RX latency up to one
      period.

config NET_IRQ_IDLE_CEILING_MS
    int "net poll task longest sleep with an RX interrupt (ms)"
    range 10 3600000
    default 60000
    help
      kernel/src/tasks/net_poll.rs (was TIMER_FREQ * 60): with an RX
      interrupt (or no NIC) and no TCP deadline pending, the longest the
      poll task sleeps; TCP deadlines (delayed ACK, RTO, persist,
      keep-alive) wake it on time on the kernel timer.  It bounds a lost
      RX interrupt: a frame whose interrupt never arrives is still drained
      within this long.  Cost: one wake per period.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transparent RPC (userspace/services/rpcsrv, userspace/lib/rpc)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config RPC_NODE_ID
    int "This node's id on the RPC links"
    range 1 2147483647
    default 1
    help
      The id the RPC server (RPCSRV.ELF) states in the Hello that opens a
      session, and the id the peer's `[peer.NAME]` section must list for this
      node. Non-zero; the node with the LOWER id dials, so two nodes of one
      pair must differ. The id is proven, not believed: the session is a Noise
      IK handshake and the peer must hold the static key the topology pins for
      its id (RPC_PEER_NOISE_PK here, `noise_pk` of a signed topology).

      No RAM cost. Read by rpcsrv from azos_limits::RPC_NODE_ID.

config RPC_PEER_NODE
    int "Node id of the peer node (0 = none: the RPC server is not started)"
    range 0 2147483647
    default 0
    help
      The built-in topology declares `[peer.fleet]` with this node, exports
      `decisions` to it (`export = ["decisions@fleet"]` on the RPCSRV.ELF row)
      and starts RPCSRV.ELF at boot. 0 declares no peer and starts nothing: the
      row exists, exports nothing, and the proxy endpoint of the peer's
      services has no one to call. A signed topology states its own classes and
      exports and ignores this. The node must be pinned to a key
      (RPC_PEER_NOISE_PK): without one the server refuses to run.

      The two nodes of a pair: ids `RPC_NODE_ID` here and `RPC_PEER_NODE`
      there, swapped on the other. Cost when set: one task of ~170 KiB of
      static buffers (RPCSRV_MEM_PAGES), either ISA.

config RPC_PEER_NOISE_PK
    string "Static Noise (X25519) public key of the peer node (64 hex digits)"
    default ""
    help
      The built-in topology pins this key to the `fleet` node RPC_PEER_NODE
      (`noise_pk` of its `[peer.fleet]` row). Every RPC session is a Noise IK
      handshake and the peer is accepted only if it holds the private half of
      this key, so a datagram that states a node id proves nothing and gets no
      session. Empty: no key is pinned, so the peer has no session and RPCSRV.ELF
      prints why and exits at start. A signed topology pins its own keys and
      ignores this.

      The node's own key is derived from the board's link key (the kernel's
      `linkkey` endpoint, provisioned like the brain-link PSK):
      `azos_rpc::chan::derive_static_key`. The public half of the other node
      is what this is set to.
      No effect when RPC_PEER_NODE is 0. No RAM cost.

config RPC_PEER_ADDR
    string "IPv4 address of the peer's RPC server (dotted quad)"
    default "10.0.2.2"
    help
      Where the datagrams to the peer go (UDP, port RPC_UDP_PORT), and the only
      source address the server accepts datagrams from. The default
      is the host as QEMU's user network sees it. The link is one datagram per
      frame of at most 1200 bytes, so it runs the same over virtio-net, a
      loopback and the in-memory pair of the host tests.

config RPC_UDP_PORT
    int "UDP port of the RPC link"
    range 1 65535
    default 7700
    help
      The port the RPC server binds, and the port it sends to on the peer.

config RPC_QUIC
    bool "Carry the RPC link over QUIC (through the rpcquic relay)"
    depends on !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      Off: RPCSRV.ELF sends each sealed frame as one UDP datagram straight to
      RPC_PEER_ADDR:RPC_UDP_PORT. On: it sends the same datagrams, on this
      host, to the `rpcquic` relay (RPCQUIC.ELF: userspace/services/rpcquic
      over userspace/lib/quic, a std ring-3 program whose row grants it the
      entropy pool and whose seccomp profile the network, nothing else), which
      carries each frame to the peer's relay on a QUIC stream of its own and
      hands the peer's frames back. QUIC adds loss recovery, congestion and
      flow control, and a handshake that proves the peer receives at the
      address it claims. The QUIC stack and its TLS are the largest parser of
      network input in the system: the relay is the only program that holds
      them, and RPCSRV.ELF stays no_std.

      The authentication does not change: every session is still the Noise IK
      handshake with the keys the topology pins, inside the QUIC connection
      (QUIC's own TLS runs on raw public keys and authenticates nobody;
      azos_quic::identity has the reasons), so the relay can drop or repeat
      frames and cannot read or forge one.

      On, the topology carries the RPCQUIC.ELF row (started at boot, restarted
      on failure) and the server binds RPC_UDP_PORT and talks to
      RPC_QUIC_RELAY_PORT on the stack's own address only. The relay takes
      everything it needs from this configuration, at build time
      (azos_limits): the lower of RPC_NODE_ID and RPC_PEER_NODE dials
      RPC_PEER_ADDR:RPC_QUIC_PORT, the other listens there for RPC_PEER_ADDR.
      When the QUIC connection is lost the relay cannot tell the server: the
      engine's probes break the session (RPC_PING_MS, RPC_MISS_LIMIT).

      Needs RPC_PEER_NODE (the build refuses 0) and a large board: the relay is
      an image of about 1 MiB with a budget of 1024 pages (4 MiB); the boot
      loader stages it in frames of exactly its size, within that budget.
      With the option off the relay's image is a
      small stub that exits. The same on every ISA.

config RPC_QUIC_RELAY_PORT
    int "Local UDP port of the rpcquic relay"
    range 1 65535
    default 7701
    help
      The port the relay binds, on this host's own address (the stack has no
      127.0.0.0/8: a datagram sent to its own address is delivered to the socket
      bound at the port), and the only address the RPC server sends frames to
      and accepts them from when RPC_QUIC is on. Different from RPC_UDP_PORT and
      RPC_QUIC_PORT: the stack gives a port to one socket. No effect when
      RPC_QUIC is off. No RAM cost.

config RPC_QUIC_PORT
    int "UDP port of the QUIC connection between the relays"
    range 1 65535
    default 7702
    help
      The port the relay's QUIC endpoint binds, and the port the dialing relay
      connects to on RPC_PEER_ADDR (the two nodes of a pair share it, as they
      share RPC_UDP_PORT). No effect when RPC_QUIC is off. No RAM cost.

config RPC_WARRANT_TTL_MS
    int "Lifetime of a warrant minted for a peer (ms)"
    range 1000 86400000
    default 15000 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 60000
    help
      The node that exports an endpoint calls the real service through a
      capability derived for the peer's session: attenuated to the call
      right, badged with the session, expiring after this long and renewed
      while it is used (when a quarter of its life is left). The pages of a
      MemObj that crossed are re-minted as a new object whose lent copy lapses
      after the same time.

      A shorter TTL bounds how long a revoked session's authority could
      outlive the revocation if the Bye is lost; each renewal is one derive
      call. No RAM cost; the same on every ISA (wall-clock).

config RPC_DEDUPE_WINDOW
    int "Calls per session the far node remembers the answer of"
    range 1 16
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      An `#[at_most_once]` or `#[once_only]` call that arrives twice (a
      resend after a lost answer, a datagram the link repeated) is answered
      from the window and not run again. A call number the window no longer
      holds is answered `NoConnection`: the outcome is unknown, and running it
      a second time is the one thing the attribute forbids. Answers larger
      than 512 bytes are not kept.

      Cost: 512 B plus bookkeeping per entry per session, reserved for 16
      entries and 4 sessions whatever this says (azos_rpc::DEDUPE_CAP).

config RPC_RETRIES
    int "Resends of a call before it is given up (idempotent, at-most-once)"
    range 0 16
    default 3
    help
      A call whose answer is late is sent again after RPC_RTO_MS, this many
      times, with the same call number; then the caller is told NoConnection
      and the session counts a miss. A `#[once_only]` call is never resent.

config RPC_RTO_MS
    int "Wait for an answer before a resend (ms)"
    range 10 10000
    default 200
    help
      The retransmission timeout, and the tick of the RPC server's housekeeping
      (Hello resends, probes, sweeping lapsed warrants).

config RPC_PING_MS
    int "Silence after which a session is probed (ms)"
    range 100 3600000
    default 1000
    help
      A session that has heard nothing from the peer for this long gets a
      Ping; an unanswered one counts a miss.

config RPC_MISS_LIMIT
    int "Unanswered probes or calls in a row that break a session"
    range 1 255
    default 3
    help
      After this many misses in a row the session is broken: every warrant and
      every re-minted object over it is revoked, the callers waiting on it are
      told NoConnection, and the peer is sent a Bye so it revokes its side.
      Any frame from the peer resets the count.

config RPC_REVOKE_BOUND_MS
    int "Bound on a revocation reaching a peer (ms)"
    range RPC_RTO_MS 60000
    default 1000
    help
      When the endpoint behind an export is closed or revoked, the server
      sends every peer that holds authority over it a sealed notice and the
      peer answers once it has revoked what it holds (the interface is no
      longer offered to its callers, the objects re-minted from this node's
      replies are revoked). The notice is resent every RPC_RTO_MS. A peer that
      has not answered within this long has its session broken, which revokes
      everything minted over it on both nodes: the revocation is bounded by
      this value on every link, and a link that cannot carry the answer does
      not keep the authority alive. Both nodes record `Rule::RemoteRevoke`.

      Never below RPC_RTO_MS (the range): the peer gets at least one
      retransmission timeout to answer the first send. A bound under two
      timeouts leaves no room for a resend, so a single lost frame breaks the
      session; keep it at several timeouts on a lossy link.

      Granularity is the server's housekeeping tick (half of RPC_RTO_MS), so
      the session breaks up to that much after the bound. No RAM cost; the
      same on every ISA (wall-clock).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Zenoh gateway (userspace/services/zenohd, userspace/lib/zenoh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config ZENOH_GW
    bool "Zenoh gateway (ZENOHD.ELF): the decision records as topics, the Decisions ops as queryables"
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for ZENOHD.ELF and the kernel starts
      it at boot. The gateway is a client of one Zenoh router (ZENOH_ROUTER_ADDR)
      over TCP. It holds one lendable endpoint capability, the call right with
      DUP on `endpoint.decisions`, and reads nothing else: no file, no procfs
      view. Its configuration is this Kconfig menu. With ZENOH_DECISIONS_KEY set
      it declares a queryable on that key expression; a query whose key names an
      op of the Decisions interface (`azos/dec/get` is `Decisions::get`)
      becomes one IDL call through a warrant the gateway asks the kernel for
      (call right only, badged with the gateway's id of the query, lapsing with
      the query). The kernel admits or refuses the call, and the answer carries
      the decision record that is the receipt. The kernel's decision records
      are published as topics under ZENOH_DECISION_TOPIC.

      The gateway holds exactly the authority its row lists and lends none: it
      is not the warrant transport between nodes (RPCSRV.ELF is). It also
      authenticates nobody: the link is plain TCP and any peer the router lets
      query a key may ask for the op behind it. The badge a call carries is the
      gateway's own counter of queries, not the requester: the ZenohID a query
      names is written by the querier and is never taken as an identity. What
      bounds that is the row (only the endpoints it holds), the kernel's
      admission of every call, ZENOH_QUERY_RATE and ZENOH_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS; who
      may query is the router's access control and the network the router sits
      on. Cost when set: one task of 160 pages (ZENOHD_MEM_PAGES in the
      topology builder) and one TCP connection, either ISA.

config ZENOH_DECISIONS_KEY
    string "Key expression the Decisions ops are served under as queryables (empty: none)"
    default ""
    help
      The key expression the gateway declares a queryable on, normally a prefix
      pattern such as `azos/dec/**`. A query names one op by the last chunk of
      its key (`azos/dec/get`); a key with a wildcard names no op and is not
      served. Empty declares no queryable: only the decision topics flow.
      The ops return decision records, which tell the network which
      capabilities were refused to whom: set it only on a router you trust
      with that. A string that is not a key expression (an empty chunk) is refused
      at start.

config ZENOH_ROUTER_ADDR
    string "IPv4 address of the Zenoh router (dotted quad)"
    default "10.0.2.2"
    help
      The router the gateway dials. The default is the host as QEMU's user
      network sees it. A lost link is redialled with a wait that doubles up to
      five seconds.

config ZENOH_ROUTER_PORT
    int "TCP port of the Zenoh router"
    range 1 65535
    default 7447
    help
      The port zenohd (the router) listens on by default.

config ZENOH_LEASE_MS
    int "Silence from the router after which the session is dropped (ms)"
    range 1000 3600000
    default 10000
    help
      The lease the gateway states in OPEN: the router keeps the session alive
      with KEEP_ALIVE messages at a quarter of it, and the gateway closes and
      redials after this much silence. The router's own lease sets how often
      the gateway sends KEEP_ALIVE.

config ZENOH_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "Longest life of the warrant a query gets (ms)"
    range 1 60000
    default 2000
    help
      A query's warrant lapses after its own timeout or this, whichever is
      shorter, and so does the authority it carries: a call the kernel has not
      admitted by then is refused. An op that declares a deadline
      (#[deadline_ns]) is called with that deadline when it is shorter.

config ZENOH_QUERY_RATE
    int "Queries served per second, at most"
    range 1 100000
    default 100
    help
      Every query is a warrant minted and an IDL call, and writes decision
      records. The ones over the rate are answered `BUSY` (an ERR reply) without
      reaching the kernel, so a remote peer cannot turn the router into a flood
      of derives.

config ZENOH_DECISION_TOPIC
    string "Key prefix the kernel's decision records are published under (empty: none)"
    default "azos/decisions"
    help
      Each decision record the Decisions object holds is published as it
      appears under `<prefix>/<rule>/<verdict>` (for example
      `azos/decisions/cap-denial/deny`), the payload the record's fixed-size
      binary form (the one /proc/decisions.bin uses). At most 8 every 100 ms;
      records overwritten before they were read are counted in the console
      line the gateway prints when its link is lost. Empty publishes nothing.
      The records tell the network which capabilities were refused to whom:
      leave it empty on a router you do not trust with that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PTP daemon (userspace/services/ptpd)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config PTPD
    bool "PTP daemon (PTPD.ELF): IEEE 1588 slave over UDP steering the wall clock"
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for PTPD.ELF and the kernel starts it
      at boot. The daemon follows the best PTP master it hears (best master
      clock algorithm), measures the wall clock against it with software
      timestamps and offers each measurement to the kernel's Clock interface
      (source PTP), whose discipline slews, steps or filters. Its row holds
      WRITE on the `clock` endpoint (the right to move time) and nothing else
      beyond its two UDP sockets (ports 319 and 320).

      The PTP domain is the `ptp_domain` of the topology's `[clock]` row
      (/proc/clock); a node whose row declares none takes no part. The master's
      time is on the TAI timescale, so a master that does not state a valid UTC
      offset gives no sample. Software timestamps are taken in ring 3 after the
      datagram is dequeued: the accuracy is the poll period of the daemon (1 ms)
      plus the receive path, not the sub-microsecond of hardware timestamps.
      Cost when set: one task of 32 pages (PTPD_MEM_PAGES in the topology
      builder) and two sockets. 802.1AS over raw Ethernet is not offered: it
      needs a packet socket, which the kernel keeps to itself.

config PTPD_DOMAIN
    int "PTP domain of the built-in topology's [clock] row"
    range 0 127
    default 0
    help
      The `ptp_domain` the built-in topology declares for the node, which the
      daemon serves. A signed topology states its own `[clock]` row; this is the
      value only when the built-in one is in force. 0 is the default domain of
      IEEE 1588.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MCP bridge (userspace/services/mcpd, userspace/lib/mcp)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config MCP_BRIDGE
    bool "MCP bridge (MCPD.ELF): the endpoints a row can call as Model Context Protocol tools"
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for MCPD.ELF and the kernel starts
      it at boot. The bridge dials MCP_PEER_ADDR:MCP_PORT over the native TCP
      stack and serves the MCP server role over that one stream (one JSON-RPC
      message per line): the bridge dials out, so a relay or the agent's host
      listens and the bridge connects. For every
      endpoint its row holds the call and lend rights on and whose interface
      the bridge's catalog knows, each op that declares how it is delivered
      (#[idempotent], #[at_most_once], #[once_only]) is the tool
      `<interface>.<op>`; the IDL op table is the schema.

      Every tools/call needs a warrant: an in-tree macaroon (the RPC
      transport's token) in params._meta["azos/warrant"], issued to the MCP
      client as a principal (a Domain id), that expires and carries an OP_MASK
      naming the op. The call is one warrant the bridge asks the kernel for
      (call right only, badged with the principal, one use, lapsing with the
      client's warrant) and one IDL call through it; the answer carries the
      decision record that is the receipt. An op that cannot be undone
      (#[once_only]) is only proposed by its tool: a human confirms it with a
      one-shot warrant bound to the proposal, and the agent commits it, once,
      under the op's declared deadline.

      The bridge is the only issuer of warrants: they are signed with keys
      derived from the image's own DICE key (the board seed measured with this
      image, never leaving the bridge). At boot it prints an operator's warrant
      on the console; with it the operator issues the agents' warrants and the
      humans' one-shot confirmations through the bridge itself
      (MCP_ENROL_TTL_MS, MCP_GRANT_MAX_MS). A rebuilt image has another key, so
      every warrant of the old one stops verifying. The link is plain TCP and
      authenticates nobody: what a peer may do is what its warrant says, the row
      grants and the kernel admits; the warrants travel in the clear on that
      stream, so keep it on a network you trust (or behind a relay that
      encrypts). Cost when set: one task of 160 pages (MCPD_MEM_PAGES in the
      topology builder), one TCP connection, either ISA.

config MCP_PEER_ADDR
    string "IPv4 address the bridge connects to (dotted quad)"
    default "10.0.2.2"
    help
      The relay or the agent host that listens for the bridge. The default is
      the host as QEMU's user network sees it. A lost link is redialled with a
      wait that doubles up to five seconds.

config MCP_PORT
    int "TCP port the bridge connects to"
    range 1 65535
    default 7448
    help
      The port the relay listens on.

config MCP_CALL_RATE
    int "Tool calls served per second, at most"
    range 1 100000
    default 20
    help
      Every call is a warrant checked, and an admitted one a warrant minted and
      an IDL call that writes decision records. The ones over the rate are
      answered `busy` without reaching the kernel (and before the warrant is
      read), so a peer cannot turn the bridge into a flood of derives.

config MCP_WARRANT_MAX_MS
    int "Longest life of the warrant a call gets from the kernel (ms)"
    range 1 600000
    default 5000
    help
      The kernel warrant of a call lapses with the client's warrant or this,
      whichever is first, and so does the authority it carries: a call the
      kernel has not admitted by then is refused. An op that declares a
      deadline (#[deadline_ns]) is called with that deadline when it is shorter.

config MCP_PROPOSAL_TTL_MS
    int "How long a proposed irreversible op waits for its confirmation (ms)"
    range 1000 86400000
    default 120000
    help
      A proposal lapses after this, or with the agent's warrant, whichever is
      first; a lapsed one can be neither confirmed nor committed. Up to 8
      proposals wait at once.

config MCP_GRANT_MAX_MS
    int "Longest life of a warrant the bridge issues (ms)"
    range 1000 604800000
    default 3600000
    help
      A warrant issued through azos.grant_agent or azos.grant_confirm lapses
      after the time asked for or this, whichever is shorter, and never after
      the operator's own warrant.

config MCP_ENROL_TTL_MS
    int "Life of the operator's warrant printed at boot (ms)"
    range 1000 604800000
    default 900000
    help
      The warrant the bridge prints on the console when it starts: the console
      is the operator. After this the bridge must be restarted to print another.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SSH server (userspace/services/sshd)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config SSHD
    bool "SSH server (SSHD.ELF): remote commands over SSH-2, public-key login only"
    default n if MICRO || BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE || PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for SSHD.ELF and the kernel starts it
      at boot, on every preset but the MCU kernel (MICRO: no TCP stack), a
      certifiable build (BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE: no remote door in a certified
      image) and the embedded profile (its row, 192 pages, does not fit the
      memory admission of a 16 MiB board beside the rest of the built-in
      topology; all three default to n): a node can be reached with `ssh`. It
      listens on SSHD_PORT on the native TCP stack (the ring-3 listening
      socket: NODE_LISTEN and NODE_ACCEPT on a Cap<Socket>) and speaks SSH-2
      with nothing downloaded: key exchange curve25519-sha256, host key
      ssh-ed25519, cipher chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com, all from the in-tree
      crypto crate. Strict key exchange is offered and enforced.

      There are no passwords and no keyboard-interactive login. A login is a
      signature by an ssh-ed25519 key that the signed topology lists in a
      `[login.NAME]` row (the kernel shows the rows to the server as
      /proc/ssh). With no row nobody can log in; the port is open all the
      same. The host key is a key of the kernel's keystore (the `keys`
      interface, Documentation/security.md "Keystore"): named, bound to this
      image and derived from the board seed, so the same image on the same board
      has the same host key at every boot and nothing is stored on the volume or
      in any image. The server never holds the secret: it asks the kernel for the
      public key and has the kernel sign each key exchange. The row holds only
      the right to open that key, read its public part and sign (READ and WRITE
      on `keys`, limited to the generate, public and sign ops) and no `linkkey`
      at all, so a compromised server can neither read the host key, nor the
      board seed it derives from, nor import or wrap a key. A board with no seed
      (no key sector) has a host key of one boot only. Its fingerprint is printed
      on the console at every start.

      A session is one channel: `ssh host command` runs one image of the
      volume (an applet of TOOLBOX.ELF, or another image the row of SSHD.ELF
      may start) with the channel as its stdin and stdout; `ssh host` gives a prompt that runs one command per
      line, with the line editing, history, ^C and ^D of the terminal server.
      Pipelines, redirections and variables stay with SH.ELF on the console.
      Port forwarding, subsystems, agent and X11 forwarding are refused. Every
      refused key exchange, login or session, and every login, is a line on the
      console and in /fat/SSHAUTH.LOG. One connection is served at a time.

      Cost: one task (SSHD_MEM_PAGES in the topology builder), two small files
      at the root of the volume and two sockets while a client is connected.

config SSHD_PORT
    int "TCP port the SSH server listens on"
    range 1 65535
    default 22
    depends on SSHD
    help
      22 is the port `ssh` dials by default. Another port (a node behind NAT
      that forwards 22 elsewhere) is chosen here; the client names it with -p.

endmenu # Network
